//! member: false

// burrowd — burrow's ssh server, for commands (B5).
//
//   burrowd [-DeqTtV] [-E log_file] [-f config_file] [-g login_grace_time]
//           [-h host_key_file] [-o option] [-p port]
//
// The flags and the configuration are a subset of sshd(8)'s and
// sshd_config(5)'s (src/sconfig, R-bw04-1). Key-only: publickey with
// ssh-ed25519 against authorized_keys under StrictModes; one session
// channel per connection, commands only; root refused always (R-bw04-3);
// GIT_PROTOCOL the one env name taken (R-bw04-4); loopback unless a
// ListenAddress names another (R-bw04-6).
//
// The processes (R-bw04-2): the listener never holds the host key's
// private half; for each connection a monitor (root) forks the worker
// (the unprivileged account `burrowd`, which parses everything the client
// sends before authentication) and the holder (which becomes the user's
// session), and only then reads the host key. wolf-std cannot hand an
// accepted socket across exec, so every process is a fork, ordered so
// that none that drops privileges was forked from one holding the key.
// Without root, or on macOS (where std cannot change a process's ids),
// the same processes run as the invoking user and serve that user alone.
//
// One log line per connection (src/slog), written by the monitor.
//
// Clean room: RFCs 4251-4254, 8308, 8709, 8731, OpenSSH's PROTOCOL notes
// and the sshd(8) and sshd_config(5) man pages (docs/SOURCES.md); no ssh
// implementation's source.
use std.env
use server

fn main() -> int {
    server.run(env.args(), false)
}
